The main reason this works is that a cluster of similar stores creates a destination. If a customer is planning to buy a specific item, like a new pair of running shoes, they are more likely to visit a location where they can compare prices and styles across multiple brands in one trip. By grouping together, these businesses collectively attract a much larger volume of potential buyers than any single store could manage on its own. So, while the competition is fiercer, the total number of customers is significantly higher. Therefore, for many retailers, the benefits of increased traffic outweigh the risks of sitting right next to a competitor.</em></p>
